1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,230
AI agents need to pay for data and tools.

2
00:00:03,750 --> 00:00:06,070
But with a separate wallet for every agent,

3
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:08,291
who keeps the whole team on budget?

4
00:00:08,550 --> 00:00:11,930
I built Orbit to give them one shared treasury on NEAR,

5
00:00:12,210 --> 00:00:14,779
with spending limits enforced by the contract.

6
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:16,100
Let me show you.

7
00:00:16,680 --> 00:00:19,700
Here are our available funds and today’s allowance.

8
00:00:20,180 --> 00:00:23,015
I’ll scroll down to the agents sharing that budget.

9
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:26,380
I’ll open research-scout.

10
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:28,780
Its key can only request payments,

11
00:00:29,160 --> 00:00:31,295
with limits for each payment, the agent,

12
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:33,166
and the shared workspace.

13
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:37,060
Let’s open the paid account check.

14
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:41,635
Research-scout paid one cent in test tokens to check which keys

15
00:00:41,660 --> 00:00:43,795
could control near dot testnet.

16
00:00:43,820 --> 00:00:47,000
The result: four keys had full control of that account.

17
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:49,540
Each works like an administrator credential.

18
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:52,160
The owner should confirm who holds them.

19
00:00:52,740 --> 00:00:55,615
We rebuilt this report from blockchain records from that time

20
00:00:55,640 --> 00:00:58,361
because the original response wasn’t saved.

21
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:02,060
Now let’s look at a separate recorded request.

22
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:04,820
It hit the operations workspace cap.

23
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:08,060
Open it, and we can see why it was rejected.

24
00:01:08,620 --> 00:01:09,955
No tokens went out.

25
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:14,800
That’s Orbit: useful work and a shared spending boundary,

26
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:15,920
in one place.

27
00:01:16,420 --> 00:01:20,098
This walkthrough uses no-value test tokens on NEAR testnet.
